<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<!-- The floor and the newest, and nothing between: a parser here is ordinary C#
		     over a generated one, so netstandard2.0 is what every consumer can read and
		     net10.0 is what a consumer on the newest runtime gets without the shims. -->
		<TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<RootNamespace>DotGram.Sql</RootNamespace>

		<Description>SQL parsers written in .gram: ISO SQL:2023, SQL-92 and Microsoft's T-SQL, all three building one tree of the standard's own constructs, with T-SQL's beside them.</Description>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageTags>parser;sql;iso-sql;sql-2023;sql-92;tsql;t-sql;sql-server;dotgram</PackageTags>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>Since 0.2.0. A script is read the way a client tool reads it, outside the T-SQL grammar, and in a text sent in one call `GO` is a word, as the server reads it. The tree compares by content: a tree equals its own reparse, and every list a node holds is a SqlList&lt;T&gt; rather than an array or an IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;.

New: SqlList&lt;T&gt;, in DotGram.Sql, the list every node holds: a read-only struct over one array, the size of the reference it replaces, compared element by element and hashed over its length and every element in order, the same on every framework. It reads as an array does — Length and Count, an indexer, foreach without allocating, AsSpan, Slice and list patterns, Exists and TrueForAll — and is an IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;. default is the empty list and equals []; a member a record may leave out is a SqlList&lt;T&gt;?, where null is not empty. It is made with a collection expression ([a, b], [.. items]) or SqlList.From(items) and items.ToSqlList(), all of which copy; there is no conversion from an array. Equality and GetHashCode of a node of either tree throw InsufficientExecutionStackException on a tree deeper than the stack has room for, where they would have overflowed it.

New: SqlScript.Read(text, options), in DotGram.Sql, cuts a script into the batches a client tool sends: ScriptProfile.SqlCmd() reads separator lines, GO n, :setvar and $(name) substitution, :r through ScriptOptions.ResolveInclude and every other command as a ScriptDirective that is reported and not run, with ScriptOptions.MaximumIncludes (10,000 unless said) bounding how many files :r reads in all, a guard sqlcmd does not have, for scripts that are not trusted; ScriptProfile.Ssms() reads separator lines and nothing else; either takes another separator word. It is held byte for byte to what ODBC sqlcmd 18.6 sends the server. Each ScriptBatch says how many times it is sent (Count), whether it is a window of the script itself (IsVerbatim) and where any position in it was written (Locate); SqlScript.Diagnostics says what sqlcmd would have said, and HasClientSyntax whether anything in the text was the tool's rather than the server's. TransactSqlParser.TryParseSql(ScriptBatch), with its quiet TryParseSql(ScriptBatch, out Statement[]) and throwing ParseSql(ScriptBatch) forms and their levels, plain and Located, read one batch and refuse a reading that stops before the batch's end.  Batch gains Source, the ScriptBatch it was read from. TransactSqlParser.Located is the same parser as TransactSqlParser called with locations on, rather than a second one compiled from the grammar, so the assembly carries one T-SQL parser instead of two. A located node's SqlSpan also says where the text in front of it begins (GapStart: the end of the token before it, or where the reading began), so a comment in front of a node is found by reading that gap alone, and a T-SQL match says where its reading stopped looking (ReadingEnd).

Changed (breaking): every array member of the T-SQL tree (SqlSyntax.cs) is a SqlList&lt;T&gt;, and every T[]? a SqlList&lt;T&gt;?: 79 under Statement, 30 under Clause, 9 under TableReference, 7 under Query, 6 under Expression, and Batch.Statements; Statement.None, Expression.None, TableReference.None and Clause.None are empty SqlLists, as is Syntax.NoNames. Every IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt; member of the SQL:2023 tree (DotGram.Sql.Ast) is a SqlList&lt;T&gt;, 183 in all: 38 under Statement, 15 under Expression, and the rest one to four to a family — TableSource, GroupingElement, GrantBody, RevokeBody, RowPattern, RowPatternClause, TableContents, DataType, Constraint, the JSON, routine, type, transform, descriptor and privilege nodes among them — and SetSessionCharacteristics.Characteristics is a SqlList&lt;SqlList&lt;TransactionMode&gt;&gt;. Reading code mostly compiles unchanged (foreach, an indexer, Length, Count, LINQ, list patterns). What breaks: handing an array where a list is taken — write [.. array] or SqlList.From(array); Array.Exists, Array.TrueForAll and other T[] methods on a member — use the list's own Exists and TrueForAll, or AsSpan; assigning a List&lt;T&gt; or an array to a SQL:2023 init property; array covariance, a SqlList&lt;Derived&gt; not being a SqlList&lt;Base&gt;; and writing into an element. The factories that build a node from a list take it as a SqlList&lt;T&gt;? where they took an array, so that a caller keeps no handle into the node: Statement.Commanded (the list form), Defined, Holding, OfDatabase, Altered, Flagged and Dropped; TableReference.Joining; Clause.Constrained, Keyed, Indexed and Ordered; Syntax.Referenced, Preceded, Sourced and Cursor. Syntax.HasOption takes a ReadOnlySpan&lt;Clause&gt;. QualifiedName.Of copies the identifiers it is given. Batch.Source takes no part in a batch's equality, as a span takes none in a node's, so two readings of one script give equal batches. Trees compare by content: two readings of one text are equal and hash alike where an array member made them unequal, two equal subtrees in different places are equal, so a table keyed by node has to be keyed by reference, and hash values differ from before. A node's span takes no part in the equality of the SQL:2023 tree either: a located tree equals the plain one.

Changed: TransactSqlParser is carried immediately (Carrier = GramCarrier.Immediate): a node is built the moment its rule is read, with no tape recorded during the parse and replayed after it, which makes every T-SQL reading faster and allocates more only on a text that is refused part-way, where the nodes of the abandoned reading were built for nothing. Trees, refusals and messages are the same as before, through the plain door and the located one. The factories of the T-SQL tree read nothing outside their arguments, so a derivation the parse gives up leaves nothing behind.

Changed: A located node's span is the range of the rule that built it, and a clause's keyword is no longer inside the value it introduces: a WHERE, HAVING or ON condition, an ELSE statement, an OVER window, an option's value and the like now span their own text where they used to include the keyword in front of them, and a member chain or AT TIME ZONE spans the whole of what it stands on. A node's Span no longer takes part in the equality of the T-SQL tree (SqlSyntax.cs): a located tree equals the plain tree of the same text, and two equal subtrees written in different places compare equal, so a table keyed by node has to be keyed by reference. A copy of a located node made with `with` keeps its span and the gap in front of it and says it is stale (SqlSpan.IsStale). ParseScript, TryParseScript and their levels keep their names and their three remaining forms and now cut a script as sqlcmd cuts it, so they answer differently wherever the old reading disagreed with sqlcmd. `GO;` is text and not a separator, and so are a GO after a statement on the same line (`PRINT 1 GO`) and a GO after a lone carriage return; the server then reads the GO as a word. A block comment does not nest for the cut, so `/* /* x */` and a GO line end a batch inside what the server reads as one comment. `GO /* c */`, `GO 2;` and `GO x` are refused at that line, as sqlcmd stops there. A last GO with no line break after it ends the batch rather than being read by its last statement as a name. A GO with nothing before it ends no batch, so no empty Batch comes back for it, and a batch of only spacing or comments — after the last GO, say — is a Batch with no statements, since sqlcmd sends it. :setvar lines are read and $(name) substituted where they used to be refused, and a :r is refused, there being no file to read. In a batch where a variable was substituted, spans are positions in Batch.Source.Text, which Batch.Source.Locate maps back to the script. The Match forms build a refusal's message when they refuse, where they used to build it when Error was first read. ParseSql no longer refuses a GO line: in one call GO is a word — `SELECT 1` and a line `GO` is a column called GO, a lone `GO` a call to a procedure of that name — as the engine reads it. Text from a file or an editor is a script, for ParseScript; text a program sends in one command is for ParseSql.

Removed: the four position and window forms of TryParseScript — (string, int), (string, ref int, out Batch[]), (string, int, int) and (string, ref int, int, out Batch[]) — at every level from TryParseScript to TryParseScript170, on TransactSqlParser and on TransactSqlParser.Located, 72 methods: cut the text with SqlScript.Read and read each batch with TryParseSql(ScriptBatch), whose positions are the script's. Syntax.Scripted and Syntax.GoLine, the helpers of the grammar rules that cut a script, which are gone with them: SqlScript.Read does the cutting, and ScriptBatch.Count is the count a GO line wrote.

Changed (breaking): SqlStandardParser reads by level, six entries where it published forty-two productions: ParseValue (a literal, signed numbers among them, or NULL), ParseDataType, ParseExpression (was ParseValueExpression), ParseSearchCondition, ParseStatement (one statement of any kind, the semicolon optional; it replaces ParseDirectSQLStatement, ParseSQLProcedureStatement and ParseQueryExpression — a query reads as a statement, to the same Statement.Select, whose Updatability says whether FOR UPDATE or FOR READ ONLY followed it) and ParseSql (statements, each ended by a semicolon or by the end of the text, as a Statement[]; an empty statement is refused). Each has its TryParse forms. ParseLiteral is ParseValue, which reads NULL as well (LiteralValue.Null); a refusal of ParseValue lists "NULL"i among what was expected. A production below the levels is read through the level that holds it — a table reference as a query that names it, a predicate as a search condition — and a statement's kind is the type of its node. TransactSqlParser reads four of them by the same names (below).

Changed: SqlStandardParser is carried immediately (Carrier = GramCarrier.Immediate): a node is built the moment its rule is read, with no tape recorded during the parse and replayed after it. Every reading through the six entries is faster, and more is allocated only on a text refused part-way, where the nodes of the abandoned reading were built for nothing. Trees, refusals and messages are the same as before. The factories of the SQL:2023 tree read nothing outside their arguments and the guards read a tower's roles, so a derivation the parse gives up leaves nothing behind.

Changed: Sql92Parser is carried immediately too (Carrier = GramCarrier.Immediate), the last of the three parsers here to be: a node is built the moment its rule is read, with no tape recorded during the parse and replayed after it. Every accepted reading through its four entries is faster; a text refused part-way allocates more, where the nodes of the abandoned reading were built for nothing, and one refused late, after a long accepted prefix, can take longer than it did on the tape. Trees, refusals and messages are the same as before. The factories it calls read nothing outside their arguments, so a derivation the parse gives up leaves nothing behind.

Changed: a node of the SQL:2023 tree (DotGram.Sql.Ast) keeps where it was written in eight bytes, the start and the length, where it kept a whole SqlSpan of twelve that most records padded to sixteen: most nodes are eight bytes smaller, and a tree SqlStandardParser builds about a tenth smaller (sql/select20 5,872 to 5,312 bytes, sql/conditions100 37,584 to 33,584). What Span answers is unchanged: its GapStart is its At, as it was for every node of that tree. The T-SQL tree keeps its twelve bytes and the gap in front of each node a located reading offers it.

Changed (breaking): QualifiedName, a name of the SQL:2023 tree (DotGram.Sql.Ast), is a value and no longer a record class: its first Identifier and an array of the others, where there are any, so that a name of one part costs the node holding it nothing but its identifier. A tree SqlStandardParser builds is about a tenth smaller, and its reading a few per cent faster where names are many (sql/select20 8,288 to 7,056 bytes, sql/conditions100 52,936 to 47,336, sql/arithmetic 1,744 to 1,520; a CREATE TABLE, whose optional names grew, 1,672 to 1,712). Count, the indexer, First, Last and foreach read the parts without allocating; Parts is kept and makes a list on each call; the positional Parts and its Deconstruct are gone, and new QualifiedName(first) and new QualifiedName(first, rest) are added beside new QualifiedName(parts) and QualifiedName.Of. An optional name is a QualifiedName?, a Nullable: `x is null` reads as before, and its value is `x.Value`. default(QualifiedName) has no parts and is no name: Sql2023Writer and SqlWalker refuse it, and the constructors refuse an empty list or a null first part. A name keeps no span: Span runs from its first part to its last where both were located, and is unknown otherwise; Locate on a name keeps nothing. SqlWalker hands out each part of a name once, after the name.

Changed (breaking): LiteralValue, a literal of the SQL:2023 tree (DotGram.Sql.Ast), is an Expression, and Expression.Literal is removed: a literal standing as a value is the LiteralValue itself, one node where it was two (72 bytes to 40 for a number), and a position that takes only a literal keeps the type LiteralValue. `Expression.Literal { Value: LiteralValue.Numeric n }` is now `LiteralValue.Numeric n`, and `new Expression.Literal(v)` is `v`. A tree SqlStandardParser builds is 3 to 7 per cent smaller where literals are many (sql/conditions100 47,336 to 44,136 bytes), at the same speed. LiteralValue.Binary is declared `new`, as it hides Expression.Binary inside the family.

Changed (breaking): TransactSqlParser hands back the tree of DotGram.Sql.Ast, the one SqlStandardParser and Sql92Parser build, instead of the T-SQL tree of SqlSyntax.cs, through every entry, at every level, plain and Located: ParseStatement and ParseSelect give a DotGram.Sql.Ast.Statement, ParseSql a DotGram.Sql.Ast.Statement[], ParseSearchCondition and ParseExpression a DotGram.Sql.Ast.Expression, and ParseScript a Batch[], in every TryParse form too. Print what they give with Sql2023Writer or TransactSqlWriter; SqlWriter prints only the old tree, which no parser hands back. It reads at four of the standard's six levels by the standard's names — ParseExpression (was ParseValueExpression), ParseSearchCondition, ParseStatement and ParseSql, with the per-version entries and ParseSelect — where SqlStandardParser reads at six, ParseValue and ParseDataType besides. The two are not published for T-SQL, since they would grow its generated parser by 1.1 per cent; they can be added later. ParseQuery is gone: to read a query, call ParseSelect and take its Query; there is no entry for a bare query without its statement's ORDER BY or OPTION. ParseSelect stays, a query statement and nothing else, which refuses any other statement where it begins. Batch moves from DotGram.Sql to DotGram.Sql.Ast, with its members as they were (Statements, now of the new tree, Go and Source). TransactSqlParser.Located says where a statement and the queries, expressions and table sources in it were written, as the range of the input they were read from; the new tree keeps no gap in front of a node, so Span.GapStart is Span.At. For now the grammar's rules still build the T-SQL tree and each entry carries the reading across to the new one, which costs a reading time and allocation; a construct the carriage has no place for yet comes back as Statement.Extension or Expression.Extension with Dialect "T-SQL" and a Kind that names it, which the writers refuse, and is never thrown. The writers print key words and date parts in capitals, whatever case the text was written in: the tree holds what they mean, not how they were spelled.

Changed (breaking): Sql92Parser builds the tree of DotGram.Sql.Ast, the one SqlStandardParser builds, instead of the T-SQL tree of SqlSyntax.cs: ParseSelect gives a DotGram.Sql.Ast.Statement (a Statement.Select), ParseQuery a DotGram.Sql.Ast.Query, and ParseSearchCondition and ParseValueExpression a DotGram.Sql.Ast.Expression, in every TryParse form too. A text both parsers read is the same tree, in the shapes SqlStandardParser gives it — a UNION, EXCEPT or INTERSECT is a list on the query it follows, a join a TableSource.Join, a literal a LiteralValue — and Sql2023Writer prints it back; SqlWriter no longer takes it. What SQL-92 has and SQL:2003 took out is SQL-92's own in that tree: DataType.BitString with BitStringTypeKind (Bit, BitVarying) for BIT and BIT VARYING — a string of bits, not T-SQL's bit, which stays DataType.Named — LiteralValue.BitString for B'0101', and JoinKind.Union for UNION JOIN, all written by Sql2023Writer.

Removed (breaking): the T-SQL tree of SqlSyntax.cs — Statement, SetExpression, Query, Expression, TableReference and Clause in the namespace DotGram.Sql, their records and the factories that built them — and SqlWriter in DotGram.Sql, which wrote only that tree. No parser has built it since TransactSqlParser and Sql92Parser moved onto the tree of DotGram.Sql.Ast; a tree is read, walked and written there. SqlComparison and SqlTruth went with it, and SqlJoin, SqlOrder, SqlLiteralKind and SetCategory are no longer public: nothing public reads them. StatementCategory stays, in DotGram.Sql, and so do SqlList&lt;T&gt;, SqlSpan, ISqlSpan, SqlWalker and the script reader.

Changed (breaking): Syntax, the helpers the T-SQL grammar's rules call, is no longer public.

Changed (breaking): Sql2023Writer is renamed SqlWriter, in DotGram.Sql.Ast: the one writer of the one tree takes the plain name, the name the old tree's writer had in DotGram.Sql. DotGram.Sql.Ast.Sql2023Writer.Write is DotGram.Sql.Ast.SqlWriter.Write, with the same members; TransactSqlWriter keeps its name.

Changed (breaking): in a located T-SQL reading (TransactSqlParser.Located) an OVER spans the keyword and the window's name or its brackets, as a located reading of SQL:2023 spans it, where it spanned the name, or what the brackets held.

Changed: the T-SQL rules build a select list's items on the common tree as they read them, and so the items of OUTPUT and RECEIVE, where each was built twice; a statement refused after its select list allocates less: SELECT a, b FROM 1,728 to 1,456 bytes and SELECT a, b, c FROM t WHERE a = 1 AND b = 2 AND c = 4,496 to 4,112, where 0.2.0, which built the T-SQL tree alone, allocated 1,024 and 2,944.

Changed (breaking): Sql92Parser refuses an interval qualifier SQL-92 does not have (§10.1: TIMEZONE_HOUR and TIMEZONE_MINUTE, which are EXTRACT's fields; a start field with a fractional precision; a precision on an end field other than SECOND, or two on SECOND; a SECOND start field; two precisions on a field other than SECOND alone) and a length, precision or scale above 2,147,483,647, which the tree holds as an int (a CURRENT_TIME or CURRENT_TIMESTAMP precision is a literal and keeps its digits). A number too large is refused with a message that does not say so: "Input does not match 'ValueExpression'.", or "Expected more input." where the text ends after it. The lists of what was expected after an interval qualifier or a number changed with them: TIMEZONE_HOUR and TIMEZONE_MINUTE are no longer named there, and neither is a precision a field may not take.

Changed (breaking): Sql92Parser reserves every word SQL-92 reserves (§5.2), where it reserved only the words of its expression layer: a word such as year, month, day, zone, with or rollback, or one of the statement level's (names, work, session, …), is refused as a name; write it delimited ("year"). A datetime field's word after a value is SQL-92's interval primary (§6.15): SELECT b DAY FROM t is b qualified by DAY (Expression.IntervalQualified), as SqlStandardParser reads it, where it was b called DAY. Every other text is accepted and refused as before. Messages change with it: where a value may be followed by more, the expected list now names the six field words of an interval qualifier, and where a name may stand it no longer names the words now reserved.

Changed: a SqlList&lt;T&gt; of one element of a reference type holds it without an array, so a list of one — a name of one part, a single column or argument — costs neither the 32-byte array nor the object: fewer bytes for every parser here that builds those lists. It compares, hashes, indexes, walks and slices as before, without making an array; AsSpan() of a list of one copies the element into a new array, where it used to span the list's own.Removed: from SqlStandardParser, the Parse and TryParse forms of UnsignedLiteral, Identifier, IdentifierChain, ColumnReference, TableName, SchemaName, CommonValueExpression, NumericValueExpression, StringValueExpression, CharacterValueExpression, BinaryValueExpression, DatetimeValueExpression, IntervalValueExpression, BooleanValueExpression, Predicate, RowValuePredicand, ValueExpressionPrimary, QueryExpression, QuerySpecification, TableReference, InsertStatement, UpdateStatementSearched, UpdateStatementPositioned, DeleteStatementSearched, DeleteStatementPositioned, MergeStatement, TruncateTableStatement, SQLSchemaStatement, DirectSQLStatement, SQLTransactionStatement, SQLConnectionStatement, SQLSessionStatement, SQLDiagnosticsStatement, DirectSQLDataStatement, SQLControlStatement, SQLDataStatement, SQLDynamicStatement, SQLProcedureStatement: 258 public methods. ParseLiteral and ParseValueExpression are renamed ParseValue and ParseExpression, and the class publishes 42 methods where it published 286.

Fixed: ten thousand T-SQL CASE … ELSE CASE … ended the process with a stack overflow in the generated reader, and are read. SqlWriter and Sql2023Writer write every tree a parser gives back, carrying the walk onto a thread of their own where the stack runs low, where a chain of about ten thousand + overflowed, and so did nested GROUPING SETS and JSON_TABLE NESTED PATH in Sql2023Writer; and the T-SQL reading's checks of a chain of UNION, of brackets and of PIVOT loop rather than recurse, where ten thousand UNION overflowed a thread of a megabyte. Equality and GetHashCode still throw InsufficientExecutionStackException on a tree deeper than the stack has room for.

Fixed: SqlStandardParser refuses a length, precision, scale, cardinality or item-method precision beyond 2147483647, and a large object length beyond 9223372036854775807, where it read `CHAR (4294967297)` as a length of 1 and `CHAR (2147483648)` as -2147483648, and threw OverflowException out of every entry on a number beyond long.

Fixed: SqlStandardParser read a string literal through a simple comment: `'a' --c 'b'` was one literal with that text, where it is the literal `'a'` and a comment. A simple comment between a literal's parts now runs to a line break, so `'a' -- c` and `'b'` on the next line are still one literal, and `'a--b'` is one as it was.</PackageReleaseNotes>

		<!-- What a caller reads in the editor. The other packages here fail a build on a public
		     member without a comment; this one cannot yet, because the tree that is leaving has four
		     hundred records and none of them is documented (docs/design/sql-tsql-tree.md). So the
		     documentation ships and CS1591 is off: a node written from here on carries its summary and
		     the page of the specification it comes from, and the day the count reaches zero the NoWarn
		     goes and the compiler keeps it at zero. Turning it on now rather than then is what makes
		     the new nodes visible to IntelliSense while they are being written. -->
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<!-- The two that say a comment is MISSING: CS1591 for a public member without one, CS1573 for
		     a record that documents some of its parameters and not the rest. Both are about coverage,
		     and both are the old tree's. The ones that say a comment is WRONG stay on: CS1570 for
		     malformed XML, CS1574 for a cref that resolves to nothing, because those are defects in
		     what a reader is told rather than gaps in what they are told. Turning the documentation on
		     found six of CS1570 in the summaries of SqlAst.cs, where a BNF production name was
		     written in angle brackets and is not XML. -->
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>

		<!-- Symbols inside the assembly, as the generator carries them: a stack trace from
		     a parser here has line numbers without a symbol server. -->
		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>

		<!-- Paths in the PDB and the package normalized to the repository rather than the
		     runner's disk, so that what CI packs can be rebuilt byte for byte from the commit
		     its nuspec names. Here and not in Directory.Build.props: it rewrites every source
		     path, [CallerFilePath] included, to /_/, and nine test classes find the
		     repository from their own file — set for everything, it failed eighty of them.
		     Only on CI, because locally it would point a debugger at /_/. -->
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

		<!-- Generated files land on disk so a reader can open what the grammars became. -->
		<EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>true</EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>
		<CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)GeneratedFiles</CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The generator, and nothing else: a .gram parser acquires no runtime
		     dependency, which is why this can be an ordinary library (docs/syntax.md
		     §6.1). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="..\DotGram\DotGram.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(DotGramAnalyzeGenerated)' == 'true'">
		<!-- The generator's own output, analysed for dead or redundant emitted code
		     (tools/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers), never packed, loaded only under this
		     property (docs/development.md). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="../../tools/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<!-- What a consumer on this framework needs: the generated parser's own methods
		     take `ReadOnlySpan<char>`, which is not in the framework there. It is the one
		     dependency this package has, and it has it only here. -->
		<PackageReference Include="System.Memory" />

		<!-- And what the compilation needs rather than the consumer: the records written
		     by hand beside the grammars have `init` accessors, and `IsExternalInit` is not
		     declared on this framework. The polyfill is a generator and emits it as an
		     internal type, so nothing reaches the package. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Polyfill" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<!-- `required` members, which the SQL:2023 tree's records declare, need two more, and
		     SqlList<T>'s collection expressions need CollectionBuilderAttribute. -->
		<MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.IsExternalInit;T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RequiredMemberAttribute;T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.CompilerFeatureRequiredAttribute;T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.CollectionBuilderAttribute;T:System.Index;T:System.Range;T:System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis;M:System.Enum.GetValues;M:System.Collections.Generic.List`1.Slice(System.Int32,System.Int32);M:System.String.StartsWith(System.Char;M:System.String.EndsWith(System.Char;M:System.String.Contains(System.Char);M:System.String.Split(System.Char;M:System.String.Join(System.Char;M:System.String.Join``1(System.Char;M:System.String.Replace(System.String,System.String,System.StringComparison)</MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- What the package says for itself. The grammars are in the repository and the
		     README points at them: a parser here is worth reading as much as it is worth
		     calling. -->
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />

		<!-- And the skill beside it, so that an agent in a project that has restored this
		     package can read how to use it without leaving the machine. The README says
		     where it is, in a comment nothing renders. -->
		<None Include="SKILL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The grammars as files the generator reads: a .gram beside the class that hosts
		     it, so that another project can point the generator at the same text and compile
		     it another way — which is what DotGram.Benchmarks does with the SQL grammar. -->
		<AdditionalFiles Include="Standard\SqlStandard.gram" />
		<AdditionalFiles Include="Standard\SqlStandard92.gram" />
		<AdditionalFiles Include="TransactSql\TransactSql.gram" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- For the benchmark host that compiles SqlStandard.gram on another carrier; Nodes and Towers stay internal. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Benchmarks" />

		<!-- For the test fixture that includes SqlStandard.gram and publishes its productions one by one
		     (tests/DotGram.Sql.Productions): the included rules call Nodes and Towers. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Sql.Productions" />

		<!-- For the tests that hold T-SQL's builders (TransactSql/Building) and the records its rules read, both internal. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Sql.Tests" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
